WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.800
Film Courage: Never let anyone tell&nbsp;
you that a story has already been told?

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:04.480
Jeff Kitchen, Dramatist / Author /&nbsp;
Founder / Consultant: That’s a fun one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:16.800
It goes back to being a bull in&nbsp;
a china shop as a writer. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:34.240
your attack as a storyteller is so key to&nbsp;
succeeding as a writer. Lazy writing, limp writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:45.360
it’s often so forgettable and the key word in the&nbsp;
entertainment industry is outrageousness. People&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:54.400
go to the movies or watch a TV show or go to the&nbsp;
theater to see something extraordinary, something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.400 --> 00:01:03.840
different, something energizing, something&nbsp;
fun or exciting or meaningful or cathartic so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:17.360
it has to do with a rebellious streak where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:26.000
you know many people have heard the&nbsp;
saying There is nothing new under the sun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:33.440
Every story's been told, There's only seven basic&nbsp;
stories or three basic stories (or whatever) and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:40.560
part of me completely understands that and part&nbsp;
of me - that just pisses me off! It’s like you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:47.840
can’t tell me that I can’t come up with something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:54.080
that you’ve never seen that will - it’s&nbsp;
like waving a red flag in front of a bull.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.080 --> 00:02:03.680
It’s just makes me mad and it brings out&nbsp;
the attack in me and the desire to shatter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:08.240
people’s expectations and blow people’s minds&nbsp;
and take them where they have never been and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:15.200
just violate their expectations,&nbsp;
violate my own storytelling rut.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:27.120
You know think of a movie like BEING JOHN&nbsp;
MALKOVICH, it’s like so off the beaten track&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:36.000
and I’m sure there was someone telling the writer&nbsp;
“Oh you shouldn’t do that. That’s not how stories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:51.040
go.” It’s just weak and it’s such a lack of&nbsp;
adventure and a lack of courage and one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:56.240
my favorite terms is ‘Attack as a storyteller.’&nbsp;
How much attack do you bring to the table? What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:03.920
are the ways that you can dazzle an audience?&nbsp;
Take them on a ride that they have never been on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:09.520
and if you accept the idea that you can’t possibly&nbsp;
come up with something that nobody has ever seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:16.400
then it kind of takes the wind&nbsp;
out of your sails in a certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:20.880
way because you’re not all that creative.&nbsp;
You can’t come up with something. It’s like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:28.720
I refuse to play that way. I go the other&nbsp;
end of the spectrum. I always want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:36.960
know what are the extremes in any&nbsp;
story idea. Just to see what they are.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:47.520
Often you will find yourself if you’re trying&nbsp;
to solve a story problem where you’ve got to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:54.560
figure out a specific thing in a story where&nbsp;
there is kind of a normal set of possibilities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:00.240
and then there are unusual possibilities&nbsp;
and then there’s extreme possibilities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:06.000
and for instance the Coen Brothers&nbsp;
say that if somebody can figure out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:11.040
our next move…if they had a week to figure&nbsp;
it out and they can figure it out in a week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:17.840
we're not going to use it. They go way above&nbsp;
and beyond shaking up your expectations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:25.120
violating your expectations. If you're&nbsp;
trying to solve a story problem and you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:31.200
a selection of possibilities for ways you&nbsp;
can get out of that situation or whatever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:38.160
one of the things I've found is&nbsp;
really fun is to not only look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:42.160
the extremes because it gets you out&nbsp;
of the stock choices in the ordinary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:49.040
but what I also want to know is just out of pure&nbsp;
curiosity what is the single, craziest possibility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:53.760
in the whole universe for that&nbsp;
particular choice? What is like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.560 --> 00:05:01.040
wildly crazy and usually it won’t work at all but&nbsp;
sometimes it's like well that's interesting. If I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:07.360
backed it down a few notches it could start to fit&nbsp;
into my arena and it’s really fresh and different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:13.680
and it takes the story in a different&nbsp;
direction and shakes up the character and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:21.120
every once in a while that single craziest idea&nbsp;
works brilliantly in a completely unexpected way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:26.800
I’ve had that happen to me where I said what’s&nbsp;
the single craziest idea and it would be like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:32.480
that and I’d be like Whoah! I’d never&nbsp;
wold have thought of that and I’m using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:37.840
that. It’s great. That’s part of attack&nbsp;
as a storyteller, just going for it and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:49.560
not just stretching the envelope but obliterating&nbsp;
it and just having fun you know? Writing is fun.

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:55.120
Film Courage: You are using analogies&nbsp;
similar to war or combat in some way. Is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.080 --> 00:05:59.120
facing the page every day like combat?

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:04.080
Jeff:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:14.160
It’s a good question because it depends in&nbsp;
many ways on who you are because if you are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:25.680
someone with the martial arts skills of Bruce Lee&nbsp;
say and then you get into the ring with pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:32.880
much any fighter you can figure something out. So&nbsp;
it’s not like “I don’t know what I’m going to do?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:39.680
I’m staring at the blank page.” It’s like “No, I&nbsp;
can make this work. This guy weighs 600 pounds so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:45.920
I’ve got to figure something out.” But Bruce Lee&nbsp;
is going to bring a lot of stuff to the table.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:51.120
So the more skill you have as a storyteller&nbsp;
and as a scriptwriter as a dramatist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.960 --> 00:07:04.720
the less intimidated you are by a normal&nbsp;
array of writing challenges. Now you really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:12.320
want to get in over your head. If it’s all too&nbsp;
easy then you are not doing your job right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:21.520
If I am starting in on a script and it starts to&nbsp;
get complex and deep, part of my brain goes Uh-oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:26.400
we’re getting in over our head here and the&nbsp;
other part of my brain goes Yeah! That’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:31.760
good. That’s where you want to be. And they&nbsp;
say that writers are like show horses. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:35.760
are not happy unless they’re trying to jump over&nbsp;
something that could harm them and I was thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:41.600
about that last night and I found actually a much&nbsp;
better analogy is that writers are like extreme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:48.400
rock climbers or something. They are not happy&nbsp;
climbing something unless it could well kill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:55.680
them. If it’s too less of a challenge they&nbsp;
are like bored. They get their adrenaline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.680 --> 00:08:00.080
from the fact that nobody has ever climbed&nbsp;
this cliff and they’re doing it with no ropes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:06.560
and they’re hanging by two fingers on a piece&nbsp;
of rock that big and they are like This is fun!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:14.160
This is life! And they don't want to do anything&nbsp;
else. They’ve even found that the combat nurses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:19.760
and medics who came back from Vietnam couldn't&nbsp;
go back to a normal job…it was too…I can’t sit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:26.240
in a corner and file papers. So they ended up&nbsp;
as like emergency room physicians and frontline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:37.440
cops…they had to be in the deep stuff because it&nbsp;
didn’t cut it anymore for them. So they more craft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:45.600
you bring to the table, the less intimated you&nbsp;
are by the material but you also want to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:54.240
in over your head. They said that Bob Marley when&nbsp;
they grew up on the water when they got bored,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:59.680
he and his brother would swim out to sea as far&nbsp;
as they could until they were completely exhausted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:04.000
and they’d tried to make it back to shore.&nbsp;
That was their idea of like a fun afternoon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:11.760
So it's kind of like that, it's like the material&nbsp;
should challenge you but you should also be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:20.240
throwing hands into your own story ideas to&nbsp;
make your job harder to challenge yourself so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:24.080
that like a rock climber is like I’m not going&nbsp;
to take that safe route, I’m going to do this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:30.480
dangerous route over here that nobody’s ever done&nbsp;
before and it may well hurt me but I can’t live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:39.520
without that level of challenge. So I’m not&nbsp;
sure if that answered your question exactly?

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:44.400
Film Courage: I think it does. So I think&nbsp;
basically you’re saying you need to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:51.440
stimulated, not bored and it’s can’t be too&nbsp;
easy and so it’s kind of like playing chess…a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:56.720
hobbyist playing chess with an expert versus a&nbsp;
hobbyist playing chess with another hobbyist.

00:09:56.720 --> 00:10:04.080
Jeff: Right and the more&nbsp;
substantial the craft as a writer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:13.840
the less it seems like How can I possibly&nbsp;
do this? It’s a job, it’s a story and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:22.960
of course it’s challenging and it’s hard but&nbsp;
like Bruce Lee facing whatever opponent he’s like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:28.000
I may get a whippin’ but I know&nbsp;
what I’m doing and can take this on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:32.400
It’s not like what am I going to do? What am&nbsp;
I going to do? It’s like no, I know exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:45.840
what I’m going to do. I just have to figure&nbsp;
out how to do it for this particular thing.

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:50.960
Question For The Viewers: Do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.960 --> 00:10:53.933
you get mad when people tell you&nbsp;
every story has already been told?

